#!/usr/bin/env bash
# ATH Detection Git History
# Builds a Splunk lookup of tune history per ATH_* detection from the detections repo.
#
# Usage (from inside the repo):
#   ./ath_git_history.sh [path/to/detections] > ath_detection_git_history.csv
#
# Assumes one splunk_saved_searches resource per .tf file, with name = "ATH_...".
# Needs GNU grep (-P) and git 2.21+ (for %cs short dates).
# tune_commits = commits after the one that created the file. Bulk refactors and
# formatting commits inflate it, so review outliers before scoring on it.

set -euo pipefail

dir="${1:-.}"

echo "savedsearch_name,tf_file,tune_commits,created,last_commit"

git ls-files -- "$dir" | grep '\.tf$' | while IFS= read -r f; do
  name=$(grep -oP '^\s*name\s*=\s*"\KATH_[^"]+' "$f" | head -n1 || true)
  [ -n "$name" ] || continue

  dates=$(git log --follow --format='%cs' -- "$f")
  total=$(printf '%s\n' "$dates" | grep -c . || true)
  last=$(printf '%s\n' "$dates" | head -n1)
  created=$(printf '%s\n' "$dates" | tail -n1)

  echo "\"$name\",\"$f\",$(( total > 0 ? total - 1 : 0 )),$created,$last"
done



  The script builds a CSV that Splunk can use as a lookup. It has one row per ATH detection: how many times its .tf file has been changed since it was created, and when it was created and last changed. That’s your tune history, pulled from git instead of from memory.

How it runs

You run it from inside your detections repo and redirect the output to a file:

./ath_git_history.sh detections/ > ath_detection_git_history.csv

The folder argument is optional. Without it, the script scans the whole repo from wherever you are.

Step by step

Setup. set -euo pipefail makes the script stop on errors and on unset variables, instead of carrying on with bad data. It then prints the CSV header row: savedsearch_name,tf_file,tune_commits,created,last_commit.
Find the detection files. git ls-files lists only files git tracks, so local scratch files and untracked drafts are skipped. grep '\.tf$' keeps only Terraform files.
Pull the detection name. For each .tf file, it searches for a line like name = "ATH_..." and extracts just the ATH_... value. This is the key that matches savedsearch_name in Splunk. Files without an ATH name, such as provider or variable files, are skipped.
Read the commit history. git log --follow --format='%cs' prints the date of every commit that touched that file, newest first. --follow keeps the history intact if the file was renamed or moved.
Compute the fields.
tune_commits is the total number of commits minus one, because the first commit is the creation, not a tune.
last_commit is the first date in the list, the most recent change.
created is the last date in the list, the original commit.
Write the row. It prints one CSV line per detection, with the name and file path quoted so odd characters don’t break the CSV.

Using the output

Upload the CSV to Splunk as a lookup table file named ath_detection_git_history.csv. Then uncomment the | lookup line in the ATH Detection Inventory Metrics search, and add tune_commits and last_commit to its table line.

Limitations to know

One detection per file. If a .tf file defines several saved searches, only the first ATH_ name is captured, and every commit to that file is counted against it.
Commits aren’t all tunes. Formatting passes, provider upgrades or bulk renames across the repo count as tunes too. A detection with an unusually high count may just have been caught in a refactor, so spot-check outliers.
GNU grep required. The -P option isn’t in the stock macOS grep. Run it on Linux or Git Bash, or install GNU grep on a Mac.
Git 2.21 or later. It’s needed for the %cs short-date format.
Header only means no matches. If no .tf files match, the script exits with only the header line, so check the folder path you passed.
